---
// TEMPORARY dev harness — delete before shipping. Renders captured fixtures so charts and copy can
// be checked against real numbers without a token in the browser.
import '~/styles/global.css';

import PreviewSwitcher from '~/components/PreviewSwitcher';
import { pageShellClass } from '~/components/primitives/pageShell';
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import type { FightDataset } from '~/lib/types';
import { analyse as analyseElemental } from '~/specs/elemental';
import { analyse as analyseWindwalker } from '~/specs/windwalker';
import idleRaw from '~/specs/windwalker/__fixtures__/idle.json';
import cleaveRaw from '~/specs/elemental/__fixtures__/cleave.json';
import phasedRaw from '~/specs/elemental/__fixtures__/phased.json';
import unbrokenRaw from '~/specs/elemental/__fixtures__/unbroken.json';
import mixed from '~/specs/windwalker/__fixtures__/mixed.json';
import poor from '~/specs/windwalker/__fixtures__/poor.json';
import strong from '~/specs/windwalker/__fixtures__/strong.json';
// The only captured pull that weaves an elixir behind a brew, and therefore the only one on which
// the Re-Origination weave renders at all. Without it the harness cannot show the case, which is how
// a weave bug stays invisible to everyone checking the preview.
import weave from '~/specs/windwalker/__fixtures__/weave.json';

/**
 * The Elemental entries, and they are **analysed here rather than stored** — the one asymmetry in this
 * map, deliberately.
 *
 * There is no captured Elemental `Analysis` to import: that spec commits raw `FightDataset`s and lets
 * `analyse()` run over them. So this page either grew stored analyses or grew a build-time
 * call, and the argument for the call is the four Windwalker entries above it. Every one of them is
 * `analyse()` output frozen at capture time, and each has already gone stale in the one way that
 * matters to a *preview*: all four have a `timeline` object with no `hasteWindows` key at all,
 * because the field postdates the capture. The Bloodlust band is undrawable from any of them, and
 * `windwalker/components/charts/__tests__/castTimeline.test.ts` patches the field in by hand to
 * render it at all. A stored Elemental analysis would inherit that failure mode on the next field
 * added.
 *
 * Analysing at build time cannot go stale: whatever `analyse()` returns today is what this page
 * shows, so an engine change is visible here on the next build instead of quietly disagreeing with a
 * file. It adds no committed bytes, the raw fixtures already being in the tree — against ~130KB of
 * derived JSON per pull that would duplicate them.
 *
 * **What the second entry costs, measured rather than assumed.** The `analyse()` call itself is not the
 * price: warm it is ~6ms a pull, ~10–22ms on the first call in a cold process, which is where the
 * "~25ms" this used to quote came from. `npm run build` cannot see it either: three interleaved warm
 * runs a side in a clean worktree land 2.54–3.33s with this entry against 2.56–2.73s without, one
 * spread inside the other. The price is the page. `client:load` serialises the whole `fixtures` map into
 * the island's props, so `dist/preview.html` goes **2 689 686 → 2 924 979 bytes**, +235 293
 * (+8.75%). Paid by a dev harness that ships to nobody, and the reason a third Elemental entry needs its
 * own argument rather than following from this one.
 *
 * `output: 'static'`, so this runs in Node at build time: no token, no fetch and no `analyse()` reach
 * the browser, and the island is handed the finished object exactly as it is handed the stored four.
 *
 * Committing the derived output would have been *allowed* had it been the better call — the three
 * Elemental fixtures come from anonymous (`a:`) reports where every actor is `Player (N)`, and the
 * standing rule is that no *named* player's data enters the repo. This is a staleness decision, not
 * a privacy one.
 *
 * `phased` first because step 22 named it: its 142.3s–192.5s submerge is the exempt-track case whose
 * in-app check was impossible while this page was Windwalker-only. It also carries the Heroism window
 * at 1 777–41 785ms, which is what makes the haste band reachable from a fixture at all.
 *
 * `unbroken` second for the band's **overlap** composite, which `phased` cannot reach: `phased` has no
 * Berserking window at all, so the one band it draws never has a second one under it. `unbroken` has
 * Bloodlust 785–40 790ms with Berserking 3 673–13 677ms sitting entirely inside it — 10 004ms of two
 * bands over the same stretch. That is the case worth being able to look at rather than a spare pull:
 * two translucent washes stacked read darker than either, and the fix chosen for that was one band per
 * stretch rather than a lower alpha, so the composite is a *drawing* decision with no route from a
 * fixture until now. `cleave` overlaps too (Time Warp 941–40 947 over Berserking 3 482–13 487) and is
 * not here *for that* — one pull is enough to look at a composite, and the cost below is per entry.
 *
 * `cleave` third, and the argument the paragraph above demands of a third entry is **not the haste
 * band — it is the target count.** Both entries above are single-target pulls and not marginally so:
 * `targets.counts.max` is 1 on each, `multiTargetMs` is 0 on each, and `bandsInPull` is `[1]` on each,
 * so `resolveTargetMode` detects `single` for both and there is no reading in which either does
 * anything else. `cleave` is Siegecrafter Blackfuse: `counts.max` 13, `multiTargetMs` 148 865 of
 * 263 233 (57.3%, against a 33% threshold), `bandsInPull` `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, detected `multi`.
 *
 * That gap is a **layout** gap and not a numbers one, which is why it belongs to this page rather than
 * to a test. Three things on the report only exist over a pull like this one: the cast timeline stacks
 * a lane per target rather than the single lane the two pulls above can draw, `TargetModeControl` has
 * four bands to lay out on one line instead of one, and every section that reads differently at three
 * or more enemies renders its other branch. None of it was reachable from a fixture, so none of it had
 * ever been measured at ~390px — the whole subject of `docs/conventions.md`'s Layout section. Rendered
 * markup is 549 340 characters against `phased`'s 364 888, and that difference is the thing a narrow
 * viewport has to survive.
 *
 * Its price, measured the same way the second entry's was: `dist/preview.html` goes
 * **2 943 573 → 3 319 162 bytes**, +375 589 (+12.8%), three interleaved builds a side in a clean
 * worktree. Larger than the second entry's +235 293 because this pull is the biggest of the three
 * (`cleave.json` is 1.2MB of raw events against `phased`'s 661KB), and still paid by a dev harness that
 * ships to nobody.
 *
 * **Not a numeric guard, and it cannot become one.** Like the two above it this entry is analysed at
 * build time and rendered; nothing here asserts a figure, and the sweep it exists for reads
 * `scrollWidth` against `clientWidth`. It is a layout specimen. What *is* pinned, because it is the
 * argument rather than the pixels, is which reading each of the three pulls detects —
 * `components/report/__tests__/previewMultiTarget.test.ts`.
 */
/**
 * The one Windwalker entry analysed at build time, and the argument the paragraph above demands of it
 * is that **the elixir weave is unreachable from any stored analysis.**
 *
 * All four Windwalker entries above are `analyse()` output frozen at capture time, and every one of
 * them predates the weave audit — so `analysis.weave` is `undefined` on all four and the section can
 * only ever draw its "nothing was captured" note. That is the same failure mode this file already
 * warns about for `hasteWindows`, arriving on a whole section rather than on one band, and it makes
 * the section the only one on the page that cannot be looked at here at all.
 *
 * `idle` rather than the cleaner weaver, because a harness wants the mixed case: four weaves out of
 * four chances, two of them closing inside the brew's last second and two not, plus a brew the fight
 * ended underneath. So the tiles, both fault sentences and every state the track can draw are on
 * screen at once. `uncounted` weaves five of six with no faults at all and would show the empty half.
 *
 * The smallest of the four raw Windwalker pulls at 1.1MB, on the page-size argument the entries above
 * are costed by, and paid by a dev harness that ships to nobody.
 */
const idle = analyseWindwalker(idleRaw as unknown as FightDataset);
const phased = analyseElemental(phasedRaw as unknown as FightDataset);
const unbroken = analyseElemental(unbrokenRaw as unknown as FightDataset);
const cleave = analyseElemental(cleaveRaw as unknown as FightDataset);

const fixtures = { poor, mixed, strong, weave, idle, phased, unbroken, cleave } as never;
---

<Base title="Report preview" description="Fixture-driven render for chart and copy validation.">
	<main class={pageShellClass}>
		<PreviewSwitcher client:load fixtures={fixtures} />
	</main>
</Base>
